today we're starting a new series called  books and puppies because  uh this  this little one has decided to join me  say say hello to willow  hello this is willow uh so she is the  baby and um she'll be providing vital  vital commentary on um neil neil  gaiman's books um before we start willow  says spoilers and um what did you guys  think of his books tell me down below  before i give you my thoughts i read  american gods by neil gaiman a year ago  and when i finished it i went to talk to  my friend benji over at the tail foundry  and he asked me because he's a big  gaming fan he said what's your favorite  part and i said i really really liked  kind of the end part where shadow goes  back to the small american town and  uncovers a trail of murders of teenagers  and children  and what happened to them it's creepy  it's unsettling it works within the  world of american gods to which benji  looked me dead in the eye and said  that's the worst part i then said that  the worst part was when shadow and  wednesday our protagonists were stuck  hanging around with these old egyptian  gods who are running a funeral parlor it  goes on for ages we spend a lot of time  getting to know mr ibis and mr shaquille  and where they came from and what they  can do and then we just never see them  again  i was reading it thinking oh these guys  must be important characters they must  have an important role to play in the  future  but they just don't they never come back  and that whole section of the book just  doesn't go anywhere  to which benji looked me dead in the eye  and he said that's the best part  ah it's like we're experiencing two  entirely different realities i then told  him that i liked the reveal that  wednesday was orchestrating this war  between the gods to revive himself and  benji said he didn't really care about  that yes hello willow uh that is that is  my hand that is not a sacrifice to you  and here's the thing benji  is right to really understand gaiman's  writing you've got to read the foreword  to american gods i moved to america in  1992. something started in the back of  my head there were unrelated ideas that  i knew were important and yet seemed  unconnected two men meeting on a plane  the car on the ice the significance of  coin tricks and more than anything  america this strange huge place where i  now found myself living that i knew i  didn't understand  but i wanted to understand it more than  that i wanted to describe it  this last line here about him wanting to  describe it to explore it is really the  heart of american gods it is a story  about exploring america about trying to  articulate it's a thesis on what america  is who these people are this culture  that is a weird melting pot of the past  and the present and the future and  its own country and yet also it's so  many other countries thrown into one  when you look at it through that  thematic lens a lot of these little  stories that don't really tie into the  overall narrative begin to make a lot  more sense you know even in an ironic  way because there's all these  subcultures there's no one culture of  america it's distinctly and inherently  and inevitably fragmented right a lot of  chapters look at how certain gods  arrived in america there was a really  vivid one for me about how this west  african god was brought over on a slave  ship by like a single girl and she kept  that alive on her own for a lot of her  life  and it really captured the struggle of  you know holding on to your culture when  so much of it is forcibly taken from you  but it also reflected how america's  culture is so fragmented it's  cosmopolitan it has no central authority  it works only on an individual basis in  this way that section with those  egyptian gods is about how old rituals  don't ever really die in america they  just evolve and change that america is a  place of constant evolution even  wednesday who was meant to be odin is  not the original odin he's not the true  norse odin he's this weird  americanized odin which is such a cool  idea you know america in this way is a  place of preservation it allows all  these things to continue but it twists  them it perverts them it changes them to  fit the landscape even the small town at  the end of the novel that shadow finds  himself in is a real american story it  is a small american rural town  that is dying or should be dying but it  isn't  spoilers  so when you look at american gods as  this vignette of capturing american  culture it makes so much sense but  i don't think i was really there for  that and  that's why it didn't really work for me  i could appreciate it on an academic  level this was intensely creative and  his prose is just makes me want to cry  because i will never write anything as  good as him  but  i i don't think i don't think i was  expecting that and so just didn't meet  my expectations of what the story was  going to be about because i wanted the  story about a war between the old and  new gods which is what he sort of sets  it up as at the beginning and i i didn't  really get that and so i bounced  off it either way gaiman is one of the  best writers of the modern day and i  wanted to give him another chance so  i picked up sandman an iconic gaming  story about  um  well it's hard to say it sometimes to be  honest it is  vaguely about dream putting his realm  back together after being held captive  for years by a cult but like american  gods it meant his  and i mean that see gaiman has a  tendency to make big narrative promises  at the start this is about a war between  the old and new gods or this is about  one of the ancient entities dream  breaking out and wreaking vengeance or  bringing his world back together but he  takes so long to get to the next plot  point that furthers that promise that i  tended to get a bit lost i was reading  this part of sandman told from the  perspective of a cat and it went on for  a long time so i was reading it thinking  this must be important i need to  remember all of this information when it  finally becomes clear about how it  weaves into the narrative i'm i'm gonna  i'm gonna see it and it's gonna be great  but um  it just doesn't really it just didn't  really tie back into the core narrative  that much or when it did it was in a  small nominal way and this happens over  and over there is this huge discrepancy  between the time that gaiman spins with  characters or stories and the actual  relevance it is to the overall plot to  the ending which  i guess i sort of struggled with as he  kept introducing more characters and  more subplots i got to the point where i  was asking myself going into them i was  going  am i actually going to need to remember  all of these am i meant to care about  this or is this going to be another one  of those little stories but again like  american gods makes a lot of sense if  you look at it through the lens of an  exploration of american culture sandman  makes a lot of sense if you look at it  as a love letter to the power of stories  and writing and dreams and imagination  because dreams ventures across the  series all these little stories show how  they affect and motivate and break  people it's why the story ends with  shakespeare's team best and why  shakespeare himself plays such a large  role and a really cool one at that i  will admit i want to be clear i love  gaiman's imagination he has just  incredible ideas and creativity that  bleed off the page with the characters  he creates and the positions that he  puts them in everything is very much  vivid you know  but  at the same time  i think i needed a bit more connective  tissue between these stories it's almost  like to me  american gods and sandman would have  worked better as a collection of short  stories each where they're sort of  connected by a general theme you know  looking at these gods and american  culture or  looking at the power of these stories  but  not making those big narrative promises  that sort of lure me into a weird false  sense of oh i'm gonna get this story and  then i get this it's pretty clear and  it's totally fine that he doesn't really  want to write about these big plot  promises they're just a way to vaguely  connect these stories together so why  not do away with them in that case why  not you know and the weird thing is i'm  not really a plot driven reader you know  one of my favorite books is annihilation  it is deeply thematic and a similar way  that american gods and sandman are  deeply characterized deeply internal and  there's there's at no point any real  suggestion that you're going to get all  of the answers uh you think you might  want it's so much about exploring the  atmosphere and feeling and the ideas  behind the novel and i i adored it i was  there for the characters experience but  i was i was never made that plot promise  this is of course  totally up front all to do with my  expectations right this is not a  reflection of his actual writing  abilities or anything like that people  bounce off novels all the time uh for a  bunch of different reasons and  i think this might just be one of those  examples for me so i read american gods  and i had read sandman and i was going  uh is he just not for me  but he's one of the best modern authors  of our day and i didn't want to give up  and that's why i am stoked that i tried  the ocean at the end of the lane the  ocean at the end of the lane is all of  neil gaiman's beautiful writing  mesmerizing themes and characters and  soft world building without any of the  drawbacks it's got a great hook a child  brings back an eldritch or ancient  creature from the mystical otherworld  and it is now trying to ruin his life  family and otherwise  and it's got an incredibly disturbing  scene in it i remember as i was reading  it he's trying to drag a  worm out of his foot like a literal worm  like it's in a hole in his foot he's  trying to pull it out it's really  uncomfortable to read and i was just  like oh oh my gosh and that was  it was done really well though it makes  great narrative promises and follows  through on all of them to a really  powerful tightly written end it's pretty  clear to me that gaiman is a character  writer and he likes writing stories with  relatively few characters like american  gods and sandman both have huge casts  but any given scene or any given little  little story segment typically doesn't  have more than  three you know  and  that exact limited infrastructure is  present in the ocean in the lane there's  really only kind of three main  characters i am not here to tell you to  not read american gods or sandman in  fact i recommend that you read them  because they are incredible and they i  really hope that they they work for you  you know uh i will tell you also to read  the ocean at the end of the lane  but i i i want to talk kind of just  about you know why we sometimes bounce  off books even when the books are  incredible you know even when the books  are  from some of the best authors of our day  they don't necessarily always work for  us even though it feels like they might  tick all of our boxes  sometimes that happens sometimes it does  but i'm really glad to have had the  experience reading them anyway what did  you think of the books though do tell me  down below stay nerdy and i'll see you  in the future

